\subsection{Token-Level Functional Specialization}
\label{sec:token_specialization}

We train cross-instruction probes (\S\ref{sec:cross_instruction_probes}): given the same visual frame with each of 10 task instructions, we classify which instruction was present from per-layer activations.

\paragraph{Image tokens are instruction-blind.}
Image\_mean instruction classification remains at chance ($10\%$) across all 32 layers in all conditions (Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}), consistent with the causal mask and frozen encoder.
Both linear and MLP probes confirm this, as does standalone DINOv2 ($10\%$), ruling out probe capacity.
RSA corroborates: instruction presence produces $|\Delta| \leq 0.006$ across all conditions (Appendix~\ref{sec:instruction_invariance_appendix}).

\paragraph{Readout token as multimodal hub.}
Trained last\_preaction achieves $100\%$ 10-way classification from L1 through L31 (LOOCV-confirmed; Appendix~\ref{sec:loocv}); untrained achieves $100\%$ at L1 but decays to $82\%$ by L31.

\paragraph{Functional implications.}
The readout is thus the sole locus of multimodal integration.
QA probes confirm richer task-relevant information at the readout ($86.3\%$ at L20 vs.\ DINOv2 $82.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:qa_probe}), replicating on LIBERO-Spatial (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}).

