\subsection{Framework Validation: Token-Level Specialization}
\label{sec:token_specialization}

As a sanity check on the experimental framework, we train cross-instruction probes (\S\ref{sec:cross_instruction_probes}): given the same visual frame with each of 10 task instructions, we classify which instruction was present from per-layer activations.

\paragraph{Image tokens are instruction-blind (expected).}
As predicted by the causal mask and frozen encoder, image\_mean instruction classification remains at chance ($10\%$) across all 32 layers in all conditions (Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}).
Both linear and MLP probes confirm this, as does standalone DINOv2 ($10\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:instruction_invariance_appendix}).

\paragraph{Readout token as multimodal hub.}
Trained readout achieves $100\%$ 10-way classification from L1 through L31 (LOOCV-confirmed; Appendix~\ref{sec:loocv}); untrained achieves $100\%$ at L1 but decays to $82\%$ by L31.
The readout is thus the sole locus of multimodal integration, with richer task-relevant content than standalone DINOv2 (QA probe $86.3\%$ vs.\ $82.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:qa_probe}), replicating on LIBERO-Spatial (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}).

\paragraph{Synthesis.}
VLA fine-tuning primarily reorganizes instruction routing rather than visual geometry: training effects concentrate at the readout ($R^2$ $+0.069$, attention $+46$pp) rather than images ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$).
This explains the visual dominance of Grant et al.~\cite{grant2026}: DINOv2 has the highest geometric alignment ($\rho = 0.692$), yet VLA readout surpasses it in decodability ($R^2 = 0.678$ vs.\ $0.634$).
Convergent causal evidence from seven independent analyses supports a functional role for L1 instruction-dominant heads in instruction-conditioned action generation:
(1)~three-condition attention gradient ($0 \to 9 \to 24$ instruction-dominant heads);
(2)~$R^2$ ablation gradient confined to L1 ($+0.059$ at L1, $\approx 0$ at L13);
(3)~trajectory divergence under L1 ablation ($d = 0.65$, $72.3\%$ token divergence);
(4)~dose-response curve across 8 conditions ($d = 0.48 \to 0.64$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response});
(5)~counterfactual instruction analysis across 4 perturbation types ($d = 0.63$--$0.97$; Table~\ref{tab:counterfactual});
(6)~per-head bootstrap CIs confirming coordinated routing ($p = 0.002$);
and~(7)~CV permutation test ($p = 0.016$).

\paragraph{Mechanistic interpretation.}
The dose-response curve reveals a concentrated routing core with steep rise through 12 heads and diminishing returns beyond 20; individual control heads produce stronger effects than instruction heads ($d = 0.051$ vs.\ $0.027$), yet 24 instruction heads collectively generate a larger perturbation ($d = 0.65$ vs.\ $0.41$).
The counterfactual ordering (empty $>$ wrong $>$ paraphrased $>$ shuffled) follows semantic distance, with L1 ablation falling between shuffled and paraphrased, consistent with signal removal rather than noise injection.
The non-trivial effect of paraphrasing ($d = 0.70$) likely reflects the base model's sensitivity to exact token sequences; a model fine-tuned on task-specific instructions would show greater paraphrase invariance.
Shuffled instructions ($d = 0.63$) produce less disruption than paraphrases, suggesting partial recovery of task semantics from preserved vocabulary when word order is destroyed.
$R^2$ measures linear decodability; action output reflects the full nonlinear forward pass. The two effects are complementary.

\paragraph{Predictions.}
Other decoder-only VLAs (e.g., RT-2) should show similar entry-layer routing reorganization given the shared causal-mask constraint; cross-attention architectures (e.g., $\pi_0$) should differ.
L1 ablation on a fine-tuned model should produce measurable task degradation on instruction-discriminative tasks.

\paragraph{Limitations and scope.}
We study OpenVLA-7B on LIBERO. OpenVLA shares the dominant VLA design with RT-2~\cite{brohan2023_rt2} and $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024_pi0}; the framework validation (C1) follows from shared architectural constraints, but routing patterns (C2) may differ across backbone scales and decoder paradigms (Appendix~\ref{sec:ood_diagnostics},~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}).
Our analyses are representational and open-loop; connecting routing reorganization to task success requires closed-loop evaluation, which we leave to future work.

